 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to authorize the state comptroller to transfer custody of funds to the natural heritage trust to be administered as a permanent endowment for the Rockefeller state park preserve",
    "basePrintNo": "S3452",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3452-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "State Finance",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S3452\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to authorize the state comptroller to transfer\ncustody of funds to the natural heritage trust to be administered as a\npermanent endowment for the Rockefeller state park preserve\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo authorize the transfer of an existing endowment, created in 1983\nthrough the donation of private funds, for the care and maintenance of\nthe Rockefeller State Park Preserve from the Office of the State\nComptroller to the Natural Heritage Trust in order to increase\ninvestment revenue.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill is the legislative intent.\n\nSection 2 authorizes and directs the state comptroller to transfer\nthree million dollars from the non-expendable trust fund of the\nfiduciary fund of the state, together with accrued interest at the\ntime of transfer, to the natural heritage trust for investment as\ndirected by this act.\n\nSection 3 provides that upon transfer from the office of state\ncomptroller, the funds shall be deposited into a dedicated account, to\nbe administered as a permanent endowment and the Natural Heritage\nTrust shall invest such funds in accordance with its investment\nguidelines.\n\nSection 3 further provides that the natural heritage trust shall\ntransfer income and earnings from the endowment account to the office\nof parks, recreation and historic preservation to be used solely for\nthe care and maintenance of the Rockefeller state park preserve, in\naccordance with the directions and restrictions established by the\nJohn D. Rockefeller III fund. In furtherance of such directions, no\npart of the endowment may be used for new capital construction, the\nprincipal shall not be invaded for any purpose, and any income and\nearnings in excess of that disbursed for the care and maintenance of\nthe Rockefeller state park preserve shall accrue to the endowment\naccount.\n\nSection 4 is the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nRockefeller State Park Preserve (the \"Preserve\"), located in\nWestchester County, was created through gifts of land by members of\nthe Rockefeller family. The Preserve is under the jurisdiction of the\nNew York State Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation.\n\nIn 1983, the John D. Rockefeller III Fund made a gift of three million\ndollars to New York State, to create a permanent endowment (the\n\"Endowment\") to be used for the care and maintenance of the Preserve.\n\nUnder the terms of the donation, the Endowment is under the custody\nand management of the State Comptroller's office. By law, the State\n\n\nComptroller is required to invest the Endowment in a very conservative\nmanner. As a result of the decline in interest rates on bonds and\ninvestment securities over the past decade, the income generated from\nthe Endowment has decreased over the years from a high of $161,139 in\n2006-07, to $4,555 in 2013-14.\n\nThe Natural Heritage Trust (NHT) was established in 1968 as a public\nbenefit corporation pursuant to Article 55 of the Arts and Cultural\nAffairs Law. The NHT is governed by a Board of Directors comprised of\nthe Commissioner of the Office of Parks, Recreation and Historic\nPreservation, the Commissioner of the Department of Environmental\nConservation, the Secretary of State and the Chair of the State\nCouncil of Parks, a statewide parks, recreation and historic\npreservation advisory board. The NHT's mission is to receive and\nadminister gifts, grants and contributions in support of parks,\nconservation, outdoor recreation and historic preservation purposes.\nThe NHT manages more than $25 million of assets, including a number of\nendowments that benefit state parks.\n\nFunds held in trust by the NHT are invested in a traditional approach\ncommonly used by universities, hospitals, community foundations and\nnonprofit organizations, generating significantly higher returns than\nexperienced by the Endowment. Based on the NHT's endowment investment\npolicy it is expected that the Endowment for the Preserve will\ngenerate approximately 5 percent annually, or $150,000 in the first\nyear (in comparison to the $4,555 generated in 2013-14 under the State\nComptroller's conservative investment requirements).\n\nTransferring the Endowment to the NHT will increase investment\nopportunities and income generation for the Endowment and provide much\nneeded revenue for the maintenance and care of the Preserve.\n\nThis legislation is supported by the Friends of the Rockefeller State\nPark Preserve, established in 1997 to encourage public participation\nin protecting and maintaining the Preserve, the Alliance for New York\nState Parks - a statewide not-for-profit organization that protects\nand enhances state parks and historic sites, and members of the\nRockefeller family. The legislative sponsors communicated with the\nOffice of the State Comptroller and the Office of State Parks,\nRecreation and Historic Preservation in developing the legislation.\nThe State Comptroller's office does not object to the transfer of the\nEndowment to the NHT.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
